1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:03,545
Compare the sequence of variation in different trees.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,645
Absolute widths can differ while shared features remain.

3
00:00:08,105 --> 00:00:13,944
Compare rises and falls around narrow rings as well as the narrow rings themselves.

4
00:00:14,747 --> 00:00:17,676
A single narrow-ring match is insufficient.

5
00:00:18,131 --> 00:00:22,269
Check surrounding variation, spacing and the whole overlap.

6
00:00:22,723 --> 00:00:25,611
A narrow ring can recur many times.

7
00:00:26,065 --> 00:00:30,657
A matching sequence of several features provides stronger evidence for choosing among

8
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:32,356
alignments.

9
00:00:33,159 --> 00:00:37,035
Narrow rings receive longer marks relative to neighbours.

10
00:00:37,490 --> 00:00:41,655
Normal rings can remain unmarked; compare feature spacing.

11
00:00:42,109 --> 00:00:45,997
Marks represent relative features, not measured widths.

12
00:00:46,451 --> 00:00:50,740
A skeleton plot simplifies selected features for comparison.

13
00:00:51,201 --> 00:00:55,640
Keep a connection to the original specimen and observations.

14
00:00:56,443 --> 00:01:00,490
Begin with securely dated material, such as living trees.

15
00:01:00,945 --> 00:01:04,833
Verify overlaps and extend the record with older wood.

16
00:01:05,287 --> 00:01:08,472
An unanchored sequence is a floating chronology.

17
00:01:08,932 --> 00:01:13,926
Connected sequences can remain floating unless linked to known calendar years.

18
00:01:14,379 --> 00:01:18,156
Inspect the evidence for anchoring the chronology.

19
00:01:18,959 --> 00:01:21,177
A: a complete sequence.

20
00:01:21,632 --> 00:01:26,889
Match 30 rings of A to reference M, 1950–1999.

21
00:01:27,343 --> 00:01:29,731
B: a sequence with a shift.

22
00:01:30,185 --> 00:01:34,455
Match 31 observed rings and locate the inconsistency.

23
00:01:34,909 --> 00:01:36,705
Teaching conditions.

24
00:01:37,159 --> 00:01:39,139
All data are synthetic.

25
00:01:39,593 --> 00:01:44,889
These short sequences practise a procedure, not real-world dating criteria.

26
00:01:45,343 --> 00:01:50,800
Pause and record both a candidate position and the sequence features supporting it.
